  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (2.5 x^2 + z^2) / 2, drawn at half height. Left-right is steeper, so overshoot appears there first: it zig-zags above learning rate 0.4 and diverges above 0.8 (2 / 2.5).",
    "Noise per slope component: SD = 1.5 × sqrt((64 − B) / (63 B)) for batch size B. It is centred on the true slope and is exactly 0 at B = 64. Noise comes from a fixed seed, so Reset replays the same sequence.",
    "Both arrows are drawn at 2× the step they show. The orange arrow is the actual next step: −rate × (noisy slope). The grey arrow is −rate × (true slope). Arrows and step length show 0 once a run ends.",
    "A run ends after 40 steps (finished) or when the loss passes 10 (diverged). Off-bowl positions are drawn at the bowl's edge, and the Loss readout keeps the true value.",
    "The trail holds the start point plus one point per step (at most 41), and so does the ghost. New run starts again from the same point with the next noise values."
  ]
}